WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.880
How do you put a number on how tightly a protein holds its partner?

2
00:00:07.983 --> 00:00:09.703
Binding is never permanent.

3
00:00:10.523 --> 00:00:13.403
A protein and its ligand keep joining and coming apart.

4
00:00:14.383 --> 00:00:17.123
Write the reaction in the direction it comes apart.

5
00:00:18.003 --> 00:00:21.203
The bound complex splits into free receptor and free ligand.

6
00:00:22.183 --> 00:00:27.223
The dissociation constant, Kd, is the ratio of what is loose to what is bound.

7
00:00:28.266 --> 00:00:31.486
Two concentrations on top and one below leave one.

8
00:00:32.346 --> 00:00:34.346
So Kd has units of concentration.

9
00:00:35.466 --> 00:00:39.286
Now pick the ligand level where exactly half the receptors are bound.

10
00:00:40.086 --> 00:00:42.126
Free and bound receptor are then equal.

11
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:49.170
They cancel. So Kd is simply the free ligand concentration at half occupancy.

12
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.040
That makes the scale run backwards.

13
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.120
A small Kd means tight binding, because little ligand fills half the sites.

14
00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:00.110
The most common error is flipping it.

15
00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:04.430
Its reciprocal, the association constant runs the other way.

16
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:06.670
For Kd, lower is tighter.

17
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:10.130
Plot fraction bound against free ligand.

18
00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:11.850
You get a hyperbola.

19
00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:15.670
It rises steeply, then bends over and creeps toward full.

20
00:01:16.683 --> 00:01:19.263
Oxygen is a gas, so we use its pressure.

21
00:01:20.123 --> 00:01:23.543
The dissolved concentration is proportional to the partial pressure.

22
00:01:24.616 --> 00:01:29.896
So for a single site, P50, the pressure at half full, is just Kd measured in torr.

23
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.510
Myoglobin is the test case.

24
00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:35.050
One chain, one heme, one site.

25
00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:37.690
Its P50 is about 2 torr.

26
00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:43.210
Predict. Myoglobin is about 98% % full in the lungs.

27
00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:46.510
How full is it at 20 torr, in working muscle?

28
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.120
Still about 91% % full.

29
00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.220
20 torr is far above its P50, so it barely lets go.

30
00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.920
So myoglobin gives up only about seven percent.

31
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:08.440
It is a sponge that grips oxygen and lets go only when oxygen gets truly scarce.

32
00:02:09.533 --> 00:02:12.313
Hemoglobin's curve is not a hyperbola at all.

33
00:02:13.193 --> 00:02:16.233
It is an S. The next chapter builds that S.

34
00:02:17.166 --> 00:02:21.466
One site gives a hyperbola, and Kd, or P50, sets where it sits.
